ice more broadly I mean you talk  about kindness which is of course  fundamental to the human experience and  yet if you look at our well right I mean  at least fundamental to human happiness  and but we see our public life get more  corrosive more vinegar ish how do you  teach kids oh no it's okay if you  believe in this thing you'll get past  that well I think we teach kids by role  modeling other behavior and by also  calling out what we think is wrong and I  think we also teach kids by elevating  stories of kids who are role modeling  kindness and that's part of what I try  to do and start now I mean one story  that I didn't know  when I was researching the book is the  phenomenon of Buddy benches and I think  about this now every day as a kind of  physical manifestation of kindness but  also a metaphor this young boy Christian  he thought his family was moving to  Germany they went to visit a school  there he saw a buddy bench they wound up  not moving but when he went back to his  school here in the States he said you  know could we have one of those and  thankfully the school supported him and  their four kids to go and sit on if  they're feeling lonely and other kids  are supposed to respect that and kind of  sit down and just talk about their day  talk about how they're doing and now we  have Buddy benches and hundreds of  schools and every single state across  America and you're encouraging kids to  get involved early even though you can't  vote and you can't participate in the  political process there's a great serie  of you at five that you wrote a  political letter to who and about what  so I wrote to President Reagan when I  was five because I had heard that he was  going to visit a cemetery when he  actually visit with my rainbow heart  stickers like Esther of good writing oh  well thank you so I you know I I didn't  think an American president should be  paying his respects to fallen German  soldiers that included Nazis and so I  wrote him this lette